::ALG2_U01_L05_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>In polynomial division, what is the polynomial being divided called? {
=dividend #Correct. The dividend is the expression being divided.
~divisor #The divisor is the expression you divide by.
~quotient #The quotient is the result of the division.
~remainder #The remainder is what is left after division.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>When using synthetic division for x - 5, what value is used? {
~ -5 #This would match x + 5.
=5 #Correct. x - 5 uses the value 5.
~0 #This would match division by x.
~1 #This would match x - 1, not x - 5.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>When using synthetic division for x + 3, what value is used? {
~3 #This has the wrong sign for x + 3.
=-3 #Correct. x + 3 is the same as x - (-3).
~0 #This would be used for x, not x + 3.
~1 #This does not make x + 3 equal 0.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>2</sup> + 5x + 6) by (x + 2). {
=x + 3 #Correct. (x + 2)(x + 3) equals x squared plus 5x plus 6.
~x + 2 #This is the divisor, not the quotient.
~x + 5 #Multiplying by x + 2 gives x squared plus 7x plus 10.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 3 #The quotient should be linear, not quadratic.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>2</sup> - 9) by (x - 3). {
~x - 3 #This is the other factor only if the divisor were x + 3.
=x + 3 #Correct. x squared minus 9 factors as (x - 3)(x + 3).
~x + 9 #This does not multiply with x - 3 to form x squared minus 9.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 3 #The quotient should have degree 1.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 3) by (x - 1). {
~x + 3 #This has the wrong sign for the constant term.
=x - 3 #Correct. x squared minus 4x plus 3 factors as (x - 1)(x - 3).
~x - 4 #Multiplying (x - 1)(x - 4) gives x squared minus 5x plus 4.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 3 #The quotient should be linear.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>3</sup> - 4x<sup>2</sup> + x + 6) by (x - 2). {
~x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 1 #This copies early coefficients instead of completing division.
=x<sup>2</sup> - 2x - 3 #Correct. Synthetic division with 2 gives quotient x squared minus 2x minus 3 and remainder 0.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 3 #This uses the wrong sign for the x-term.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 3 #The constant term should be -3.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>3</sup> + 2x<sup>2</sup> - 5x - 6) by (x + 1). {
~x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 5 #This copies coefficients rather than dividing.
=x<sup>2</sup> + x - 6 #Correct. Multiplying (x + 1)(x squared + x - 6) gives the original polynomial.
~x<sup>2</sup> - x - 6 #This has the wrong sign for the x-term.
~x<sup>2</sup> + x + 6 #The constant term should be -6.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>3</sup> + 4x<sup>2</sup> + x - 6) by (x + 2). {
~x<sup>2</sup> + 4x + 1 #This copies coefficients and ignores division.
=x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 3 #Correct. Synthetic division with -2 gives quotient x squared plus 2x minus 3.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 2x - 3 #This uses the wrong sign for the x-term.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 2x + 3 #The constant term should be -3.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (2x<sup>3</sup> + 3x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 4) by (x + 2). {
~2x<sup>2</sup> - x - 3 #This leaves out the remainder.
=2x<sup>2</sup> - x - 3 + 10/(x + 2) #Correct. The quotient is 2x squared minus x minus 3 with remainder 10.
~2x<sup>2</sup> + 7x - 19 #This uses the wrong synthetic value.
~2x<sup>2</sup> + 3x - 5 + 4/(x + 2) #This copies coefficients instead of dividing.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>3</sup> + x + 1) by (x + 1). {
~x<sup>2</sup> + x + 1 #This does not account for the missing x squared term.
=x<sup>2</sup> - x + 2 - 1/(x + 1) #Correct. Synthetic division with -1 gives quotient x squared minus x plus 2 and remainder -1.
~x<sup>2</sup> - x + 2 + 1/(x + 1) #The quotient is correct, but the remainder sign is wrong.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 1 #This is not the quotient from division by x + 1.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>3</sup> - 8) by (x - 2). {
~x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 4 #This has the wrong sign for the middle term.
=x<sup>2</sup> + 2x + 4 #Correct. x cubed minus 8 equals (x - 2)(x squared + 2x + 4).
~x<sup>2</sup> + 4 #This leaves out the middle term.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 4 #The constant term should be positive 4.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>3</sup> + 0x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 5) by (x - 1). {
~x<sup>2</sup> - 4 + 5/(x - 1) #This skips the zero x squared coefficient.
=x<sup>2</sup> + x - 3 + 2/(x - 1) #Correct. Synthetic division with 1 gives quotient x squared plus x minus 3 and remainder 2.
~x<sup>2</sup> - x - 3 + 2/(x - 1) #The x-term sign is incorrect.
~x<sup>2</sup> + x - 3 - 2/(x - 1) #The quotient is correct, but the remainder sign is wrong.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>What does a remainder of 0 tell you after dividing a polynomial by x - c? {
=x - c is a factor of the polynomial. #Correct. A zero remainder means the division is exact.
~x - c is not a factor of the polynomial. #A nonzero remainder would show that it is not a factor.
~The quotient must be constant. #The quotient degree depends on the dividend, not only the remainder.
~The polynomial must be linear. #A zero remainder can occur for polynomials of many degrees.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>If (x<sup>3</sup> - 4x<sup>2</sup> + x + 6) divided by (x - 2) has quotient x<sup>2</sup> - 2x - 3 and remainder 0, which statement is true? {
~x + 2 is a factor. #The divisor is x - 2, not x + 2.
=x - 2 is a factor. #Correct. A zero remainder means the divisor x - 2 is a factor.
~The quotient is the remainder. #The quotient and remainder are different parts of the division result.
~The polynomial has no factors. #The division result shows at least one factor.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>3</sup> - 6x<sup>2</sup> + 11x - 6) by (x - 1). {
=x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 6 #Correct. Synthetic division with 1 gives quotient x squared minus 5x plus 6 and remainder 0.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 5x + 6 #The signs in the quotient are incorrect.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 6x + 11 #This copies coefficients instead of completing division.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 5x - 6 #The constant term should be positive 6.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>3</sup> - 6x<sup>2</sup> + 11x - 6) by (x - 2). {
~x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 6 #This is the quotient for division by x - 1.
=x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 3 #Correct. Synthetic division with 2 gives quotient x squared minus 4x plus 3 and remainder 0.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 4x + 3 #The signs are incorrect.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 4x - 3 #The constant term should be positive 3.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>3</sup> - 6x<sup>2</sup> + 11x - 6) by (x - 3). {
~x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 3 #This is the quotient for division by x - 2.
=x<sup>2</sup> - 3x + 2 #Correct. Synthetic division with 3 gives quotient x squared minus 3x plus 2 and remainder 0.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 3x + 2 #The signs in the quotient are incorrect.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 3x - 2 #The constant term should be positive 2.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (2x<sup>3</sup> - 3x<sup>2</sup> - 8x + 12) by (x - 2). {
~2x<sup>2</sup> - 7x + 6 #This is the quotient for division by x + 2.
=2x<sup>2</sup> + x - 6 #Correct. Synthetic division with 2 gives quotient 2x squared plus x minus 6.
~2x<sup>2</sup> - 3x - 8 #This copies coefficients instead of dividing.
~2x<sup>2</sup> + x + 6 #The constant term should be -6.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (2x<sup>3</sup> - 3x<sup>2</sup> - 8x + 12) by (x + 2). {
=2x<sup>2</sup> - 7x + 6 #Correct. Synthetic division with -2 gives quotient 2x squared minus 7x plus 6.
~2x<sup>2</sup> + x - 6 #This is the quotient for division by x - 2.
~2x<sup>2</sup> - 3x - 8 #This copies coefficients instead of dividing.
~2x<sup>2</sup> + 7x + 6 #The x-term sign is incorrect.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (3x<sup>3</sup> + 2x<sup>2</sup> - x + 4) by (x - 1). {
~3x<sup>2</sup> + 5x + 4 #This leaves out the nonzero remainder.
=3x<sup>2</sup> + 5x + 4 + 8/(x - 1) #Correct. The quotient is 3x squared plus 5x plus 4, and the remainder is 8.
~3x<sup>2</sup> - x + 4 + 8/(x - 1) #This does not complete the division correctly.
~3x<sup>2</sup> + 5x + 4 - 8/(x - 1) #The quotient is correct, but the remainder sign is wrong.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression correctly checks a division result with divisor d(x), quotient q(x), and remainder r? {
~p(x) = d(x) + q(x) + r #The divisor and quotient must be multiplied, not only added.
=p(x) = d(x)q(x) + r #Correct. The dividend equals divisor times quotient plus remainder.
~p(x) = q(x) - d(x) + r #This does not represent division.
~p(x) = r/(d(x)q(x)) #The remainder is added after multiplying divisor and quotient.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>If a cubic polynomial is divided by a linear polynomial, what degree should the quotient usually have? {
~0 #A constant quotient would usually occur when a linear polynomial is divided by a linear polynomial.
~1 #A linear quotient would usually occur when a quadratic is divided by a linear polynomial.
=2 #Correct. Degree 3 divided by degree 1 gives a quotient of degree 2.
~4 #Division by a linear polynomial lowers the degree instead of raising it.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>4</sup> - 1) by (x - 1). {
~x<sup>3</sup> - x<sup>2</sup> + x - 1 #This sign pattern does not multiply back to x to the fourth minus 1 with divisor x - 1.
=x<sup>3</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> + x + 1 #Correct. x to the fourth minus 1 equals (x - 1)(x cubed plus x squared plus x plus 1).
~x<sup>3</sup> + 1 #This leaves out middle terms needed for the product.
~x<sup>2</sup> + x + 1 #This quotient has the wrong degree.
}

::ALG2_U01_L05_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L05_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.3<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>4</sup> + 0x<sup>3</sup> - 2x<sup>2</sup> + 0x + 1) by (x + 1). {
~x<sup>3</sup> - 2x + 1 #This skips needed zero-coefficient positions.
=x<sup>3</sup> - x<sup>2</sup> - x + 1 #Correct. Synthetic division with -1 gives quotient x cubed minus x squared minus x plus 1 and remainder 0.
~x<sup>3</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> - x - 1 #This uses the wrong sign pattern.
~x<sup>3</sup> - x<sup>2</sup> + x - 1 #The signs of the final two terms are incorrect.
}
